        </h2>

        <div class="max-w-none space-y-6">
            <div>
                <h3 class="qt-h3 mb-3">What is a Car Loan?</h3>
                <p>A car loan (also called auto loan or vehicle loan) is a secured loan provided by banks and NBFCs
                    to help you purchase a new or used car. You repay the loan through fixed monthly installments
                    (EMIs) over 1-7 years. Banks typically finance 80-90% of the on-road price for new cars and
                    65-75% for used cars.</p>
            </div>

            <div>
                <h3 class="qt-h3 mb-3">How car loan interest rates are set</h3>
                <div class="qt-card is-loans space-y-3">
                    <p>A car loan rate depends on the lender, your credit score, your income and employment type, the loan amount and the loan-to-value ratio. Ask whether the quoted rate is fixed for the whole tenure or floating. A floating rate moves with the lender's benchmark, while a fixed rate gives a predictable EMI.</p>
                    <p>Used-car loans usually cost more than new-car loans because the car is worth less as security and its value is harder for the lender to judge. Older cars and longer tenures tend to push the rate up further.</p>
                    <p>Lenders revise rates often and run festive or dealer-linked offers, so compare current written quotes from two or three lenders rather than relying on a published table, including older versions of this page.</p>
                </div>
            </div>

